🧠 **SUGGESTION: long positional parameter lists on the new orchestration helpers**

The gh-1220 extraction introduces several helpers with 7–8 loose positional parameters:

- `postReworkStartedCommentOrSkip(prAnchor, tracker, ticketKey, customParams, prDetails, branchName, conflictFiles, failedChecks)` — 8
- `postOneThreadReply(scm, pullRequestId, item, lookup, outputOptions, auditLine, addressed, state)` — 8
- `writeReworkInputContext(inputFolder, config, scm, pr, prDetails, branchName, baseBranch)` — 7

Several adjacent parameters share types (`tracker`/`ticketKey`/`customParams` strings+objects, `prDetails`/`branchName`), so a transposed argument compiles and fails only at runtime deep inside a leg. The extraction is behavior-identical (verified line-by-line against the former inline bodies), so this is pure polish: consider grouping the per-leg context into one object, e.g.

```js
// preCliReworkSetup.js
function writeReworkInputContext(ctx) {
    // ctx = { inputFolder, config, scm, pr, prDetails, branchName, baseBranch }
    const headSha = (ctx.prDetails.head && ctx.prDetails.head.sha) || null;
    ...
}
```

Same pattern the repo already uses in `enrichReworkInputStep(params, actualParams, { inputFolder, ticketKey, repo, headSha, baseBranch })` — an options object for the tail parameters would keep call sites self-describing. Not blocking; fine to defer to a follow-up.

🧠 **SUGGESTION: comparator has no equal-keys case — document or fix the tie semantics**

```js
/** Oldest-first comparator over concluded reviews (equal timestamps never drop an entry). */
function compareConcludedReviewsBySubmittedAt(a, b) {
    return reviewSubmittedAt(a) < reviewSubmittedAt(b) ? -1 : 1;
}
```

For **equal** timestamps this returns `1` ("b before a") instead of `0`. Nothing drops an entry either way — no JS `sort` comparator removes elements — so the new doc-comment rationale is misleading. The ordering only happens to preserve input order on ties because ES2019+ `Array.prototype.sort` is stable; under this comparator a stable sort may still invert two equal-key entries relative to input order (it treats "a > b" as "take b first").

This is byte-identical to the former inline comparator (behavior preserved, and the new characterization test pins the tie case), so it is polish only:

```js
function compareConcludedReviewsBySubmittedAt(a, b) {
    var ka = reviewSubmittedAt(a), kb = reviewSubmittedAt(b);
    return ka < kb ? -1 : (ka > kb ? 1 : 0);   // proper total order; stability keeps input order on ties
}
```

…and reword the comment to "ties keep input order (stable sort)". The pinned test `equal submitted_at keeps both entries` stays green either way.

